          "dimension": "Operational / Key Management",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "The March 2026 exploit was not a smart-contract bug but a compromised contractor GitHub credential that exposed an AWS signing key, enabling unauthorized minting of 80M USR — a purely operational failure.",
          "source": "https://www.chainalysis.com/blog/lessons-from-the-resolv-hack/"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Centralization",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "A single off-chain signing key controlled the ability to mint unbacked stablecoins, meaning no on-chain governance or multisig threshold prevented the $25M loss.",
          "source": "https://resolv.xyz/blog/resolv-postmortem-march-22-2026-incident"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Liquidity / Contagion",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "USR depegged sharply post-exploit and exposure propagated through Morpho vaults (Gauntlet, MEV Capital, Steakhouse USDC), Fluid lending markets, and briefly threatened Aave liquidity providers.",
          "source": "https://www.spglobal.com/ratings/en/regulatory/article/digital-assets-brief-defi-hacks-underscore-the-significance-of-operational-security-and-risk-management-s101686023"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Smart Contract",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Fluid's automated borrowing ceilings triggered correctly and paused USR markets, limiting on-chain smart-contract damage — the vulnerability was off-chain credential management, not contract logic.",
